Burgundy is not one colour. It is a family, and most people wearing <strong>burgundy fall nails</strong> this season have never been told which member of that family is actually on their hands. If you have saved a dozen burgundy nail photos to a folder, there is a good chance half of them are maroon and one or two are oxblood. That gap between what you saved and what you asked for is exactly what makes the difference matter, and it is why so many women end up back at the salon pointing at a photo that isn't the shade they walked in wanting. This guide sorts the naming confusion out first, then gets specific about undertone, shape, and finish, so your <strong>burgundy fall nails</strong> land as the exact shade you meant, not another 50 photos to scroll through.

