## What's the Difference Between Burgundy, Maroon, and Wine Nails?

Burgundy has purple undertones. Maroon leans brown. Wine sits warm and red, closer to the fruit than the flower. Those three sentences are the entire answer, and most articles bury it under thirty photos instead of leading with it.

**Burgundy** is a deep red with a distinct purple or blue cast underneath, the shade named for the French wine region and the colour that reads as elegant, cool, and a little severe in the best way. **Maroon** occupies the same depth of colour but pulls toward brown, which gives it a warmer, more rustic, more autumnal feeling on the hand. **Wine** is looser as a term, generally used for anything in this family with a noticeably red, less purple lean, the shade you'd point to on an actual glass of red rather than the bottle's label. Then there's **oxblood**, the near-black end of the spectrum, a brown-red so deep it can look almost black in low light, and the most dramatic option of the four.

None of this is pedantry. If you show a nail tech a maroon photo and ask for burgundy, you'll get a shade with more purple in it than you pictured, and it will read differently against your skin. Fall's other dominant nail trend, a warm brown-based mani, sits closer to maroon than true <strong>burgundy fall nails</strong>, which is part of why the two get confused so often this time of year. Once you can name the difference, choosing between them stops being guesswork.

