## The Best Nail Shapes for Burgundy This Fall

Almond is not the only shape that works with <strong>burgundy fall nails</strong>, but it is the one that makes the colour look considered rather than incidental. The tapered point softens a colour this saturated, and the elongating effect keeps a dark shade from reading heavy on the hand. It's the safest, most universally flattering pairing on this list, and it holds at almost any length, which matters if you're not growing your nails out for the season.

Square is the sharper choice, and it earns that edge honestly. The flat tip and clean corners give burgundy a graphic, architectural quality that almond simply doesn't have. On shorter nails especially, a square burgundy set looks deliberate rather than like a placeholder length. Coffin sits between the two aesthetically but asks more of your nails structurally: you need enough length for the taper-to-flat-tip silhouette to actually register, or the shape collapses into a blunt square and the drama is lost. On longer acrylic or gel extensions, though, coffin gives burgundy a runway quality that neither almond nor square attempts.

Oval is worth naming too, even though it rarely gets the spotlight. It's the most classic, most occasion-agnostic option, and a short oval in a fully saturated burgundy is one of the more corporate-friendly shapes on this list, which becomes relevant a few sections from now.

