## Is Burgundy Office-Appropriate? Where the Line Actually Is

Burgundy is not too dark for the office. It is, in fact, one of the safer dark shades to bring into a conservative workplace, and the hesitation most people feel about it is worth naming and then setting aside.

The instinct to worry usually comes from lumping burgundy in with black or true red, both of which carry more polarising reputations. Burgundy doesn't carry that baggage. It reads as muted and considered rather than loud, which is exactly why it shows up so often on the hands of women in law, finance, and medicine, industries with genuinely conservative dress codes. The actual line isn't about the colour family at all. It's about finish and upkeep. A glossy, fully opaque burgundy in good condition reads as polished in nearly any professional setting. A chipped one, regardless of shade, reads as neglected. That's the whole test, and it applies just as much to a pale nude as it does to the deepest oxblood.

For burgundy specifically, the short version holds: this is a safe choice, condition permitting, in workplaces from creative studios to conservative law firms alike.

